<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-2-volumes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Volumes (Disks and Washers)</title>
  <!-- Sessions 15-16 . Thu Oct 15 and Tue Oct 20 . Stewart Sec. 6.2 . Quiz 5 on Oct 20 (Skills 18-19).
       Built from the Section 6.2 instructor handout ("From Area to Volume") in the
       conversational handout voice. Written 2026-10-22.
       Big idea of the section: V = integral of A(x) dx. Disks and washers are just two
       ways of finding A(x). Do not lead with the disk formula.
       The 3D animation lives in assets/volumes/spin-the-slice.html (three.js, loaded from
       jsdelivr). It is the same page as the Claude artifact "Spin the Slice". -->

  <introduction>
    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-question-volumes">
      <title>Today's Question</title>
      <p>
        We found area by adding up many thin rectangles. Can we find a
        <em>volume</em> by adding up many thin pieces?
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>
      Spoiler: yes. And the integral we get has exactly the same shape as the
      one for area. Only one ingredient changes.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-what-we-know">
    <title>Start with something we already know</title>

    <p>
      Take a region under <m>y = f(x)</m> from <m>x = a</m> to <m>x = b</m>.
      Cut it into thin rectangles. A typical rectangle has height <m>f(x)</m>
      and width <m>\Delta x</m>, so
      <md>
        \Delta A \approx f(x)\,\Delta x.
      </md>
      Add them up and let the rectangles get thinner:
      <md>
        A = \int_a^b f(x)\,dx.
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-what-are-we-adding" label="check-what-are-we-adding">
      <title>What are we actually adding?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In <m>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx</m>, what are the tiny things being added together?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p>Heights <m>f(x)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Not quite. A height alone has no area. Something has to multiply it.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Areas of thin rectangles, <m>f(x)\,\Delta x</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Yes. Height times width. Keep that picture in your head.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p>Widths <m>\Delta x</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Adding only the widths gives <m>b - a</m>. That is the length of the interval, not the area.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-slab">
    <title>What is a slab?</title>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>1. A familiar prism</title>
      <p>
        How do you find the volume of a box, or any prism with straight sides?
        You need two things: the area of the face, <m>A</m>, and the thickness,
        <m>h</m>. Then
        <md>
          V = (\text{area of face})(\text{thickness}) = Ah.
        </md>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>2. Make it very thin</title>
      <p>
        Now picture one very thin piece of a more complicated solid. Its face
        at position <m>x</m> has area <m>A(x)</m>. Its thickness is
        <m>\Delta x</m>. So
        <md>
          \Delta V \approx A(x)\,\Delta x.
        </md>
        Why only <em>approximately</em>? The solid changes a little from one
        side of the slab to the other, so the slab is not a perfect prism.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <exercise xml:id="check-thinner-slab" label="check-thinner-slab">
      <title>Predict</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If the slab gets thinner, does <m>A(x)\,\Delta x</m> become a better
          or a worse estimate of the slab's volume?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Better</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. A very thin slab barely changes from one side to the other, so it is almost exactly a prism.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p>Worse</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Think about the Riemann rectangles in <xref ref="sec-5-1-areas-distances"/>. Thinner rectangles gave better estimates. Same thing here.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>3. Compare 2D and 3D</title>
      <p>
        Put the two ideas side by side.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell>Area</cell>
        <cell>Volume</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\Delta A \approx f(x)\,\Delta x</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\Delta V \approx A(x)\,\Delta x</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>height <m>f(x)</m></cell>
        <cell>cross-sectional area <m>A(x)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>width <m>\Delta x</m></cell>
        <cell>thickness <m>\Delta x</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>

    <p>
      What changed? The height <m>f(x)</m> was replaced by the area of a
      cross-section, <m>A(x)</m>. What stayed the same? The <m>\Delta x</m>.
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>4. Many slabs, then thinner slabs</title>
      <p>
        Cut the solid from <m>x = a</m> to <m>x = b</m> into <m>n</m> slabs and add:
        <md>
          V \approx A(x_1^*)\,\Delta x + A(x_2^*)\,\Delta x + \cdots + A(x_n^*)\,\Delta x
          = \sum_{i=1}^{n} A(x_i^*)\,\Delta x.
        </md>
        That is a Riemann sum. The structure is identical to the one for area.
        As <m>\Delta x \to 0</m>, it becomes a definite integral.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-volume-main-idea">
      <title>The big idea of this section</title>
      <p>
        <md>
          V = \int_a^b A(x)\,dx
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The hard part of a volume problem is usually not the integration. It is
        figuring out <m>A(x)</m>, the area of a typical cross-section.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-spin">
    <title>What happens when a rectangle spins?</title>

    <p>
      Here is where a lot of the solids in this section come from. Take a flat
      region, and spin it around an axis. It sweeps out a solid.
    </p>
    <p>
      That sounds hard to picture. So do not try to picture the whole solid
      yet. Watch <em>one</em> thin rectangle.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-spin-the-slice">
      <caption>
        Spin the Slice. One thin rectangle spins into a disk or a washer. All of
        them together sweep out the solid.
      </caption>
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-spin-the-slice" label="interactive-spin-the-slice"
                   iframe="volumes/spin-the-slice.html" width="100%" aspect="4:9">
        <instructions>
          <p>
            Click through steps 1 to 5. Step 3 will not spin until you predict:
            pick what the orange rectangle will become, and drag the purple line
            to where you think its outer edge is. Then lock in and watch. Drag
            the picture to look around the solid, and switch between the four
            examples. When you are done, try the Match game tab: a solid spins,
            and you pick which flat region and axis made it.
          </p>
        </instructions>
      </interactive>
    </figure>
    <p>
      <url href="https://gyagr7.github.io/Calculus-I/external/volumes/spin-the-slice.html"
           visual="gyagr7.github.io/Calculus-I/external/volumes/spin-the-slice.html">Open animation in a new tab</url>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-rectangle-becomes" label="check-rectangle-becomes">
      <title>What do you notice?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the <q>Disk</q> example, the rectangle stands on the <m>x</m>-axis
          and reaches up to <m>y = x^2</m>. When it spins about the
          <m>x</m>-axis, what does it become, and what is its radius?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          A thin <term>disk</term>. Its radius is the height of the rectangle,
          <m>r(x) = x^2</m>, because that is how far its top edge is from the axis.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      Now give the idea a name. If a rectangle from the axis up to
      <m>y = f(x)</m> spins about the <m>x</m>-axis, it makes a disk with
      radius <m>r(x) = f(x)</m>. The face of that disk is a circle, so
      <md>
        A(x) = \pi[r(x)]^2 = \pi[f(x)]^2
      </md>
      and the big idea gives
      <md>
        V = \int_a^b \pi[f(x)]^2\,dx.
      </md>
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-where-square">
      <title>Where did the square come from?</title>
      <p>
        Not from <q>it is a volume problem.</q> It comes from the geometry:
        the area of a circle is <m>\pi r^2</m>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <warning xml:id="warning-disk-not-general">
      <p>
        <m>V = \pi\int r^2\,dx</m> only works because the cross-section happens
        to be a circle. The general rule is still <m>V = \int A(x)\,dx</m>.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-we-do-disk">
    <title>We do: build the integral from the picture</title>

    <p>
      Rotate the region bounded by <m>y = x^2</m>, <m>y = 0</m>, <m>x = 0</m>,
      and <m>x = 2</m> about the <m>x</m>-axis. (This is the <q>Disk</q>
      example in <xref ref="fig-spin-the-slice"/>.)
    </p>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li><p><alert>Radius.</alert> The slice reaches from the axis to the curve: <m>r(x) = x^2</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Area.</alert> <m>A(x) = \pi(x^2)^2 = \pi x^4</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Limits.</alert> <m>0 \le x \le 2</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p><alert>Integral.</alert> <m>V = \int_0^2 \pi x^4\,dx</m>.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-disk-volume" label="check-disk-volume">
      <title>Try it</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\int_0^2 \pi x^4\,dx</m>. Give a decimal to two places.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>V \approx</m> <fillin answer="20.11" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.01"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>\pi\left[\frac{x^5}{5}\right]_0^2 = \frac{32\pi}{5} \approx 20.11</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test>
            <numcmp value="6.4" tolerance="0.01"/>
            <feedback><p>Close. You found <m>\frac{32}{5}</m>. Where did the <m>\pi</m> go?</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-washers">
    <title>What if there is a hole?</title>

    <p>
      Now take a region trapped between two curves, <m>y = R(x)</m> on the
      outside and <m>y = r(x)</m> on the inside, with
      <m>R(x) \ge r(x) \ge 0</m>. Spin it about the <m>x</m>-axis.
    </p>
    <p>
      Switch the animation to the <q>Washer</q> example and run step 3. The
      rectangle no longer touches the axis. There is a gap under it, and that
      gap spins into a hole. The cross-section is a <term>washer</term>: a big
      disk with a smaller disk removed.
    </p>
    <p>
      So
      <md>
        A(x) = \text{outer disk} - \text{inner disk} = \pi R^2 - \pi r^2 = \pi\left(R^2 - r^2\right)
      </md>
      and
      <md>
        V = \pi\int_a^b \left([R(x)]^2 - [r(x)]^2\right)dx.
      </md>
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-subtract-areas">
      <title>Remember</title>
      <p>
        Subtract <em>areas</em>, not radii. <m>\pi(R - r)^2</m> is not a washer.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise xml:id="check-washer-not" label="check-washer-not">
      <title>Quick check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A washer has <m>R = 3</m> and <m>r = 2</m>. What is its area?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p><m>\pi</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>That is <m>\pi(3 - 2)^2</m>, the area of a disk of radius 1. A washer is not a small disk.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>5\pi</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Yes. <m>9\pi - 4\pi = 5\pi</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p><m>9\pi</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>That is the whole outer disk. You still need to remove the hole.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-we-do-washer">
    <title>We do: which radius is which?</title>

    <p>
      Rotate the region between <m>y = x</m> and <m>y = x^2</m>,
      <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, about the <m>x</m>-axis.
    </p>
    <p>
      At a typical <m>x</m>, the line <m>y = x</m> is <em>farther from the axis</em>,
      so it gives the outer radius. The curve <m>y = x^2</m> is closer, so it
      gives the inner radius:
      <md>
        R(x) = x, \qquad r(x) = x^2.
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Notice the phrase. Not <q><m>x</m> is bigger than <m>x^2</m>.</q> The
      reason is distance from the axis. That matters in a minute.
    </p>
    <p>
      <md>
        A(x) = \pi\left[x^2 - (x^2)^2\right] = \pi\left(x^2 - x^4\right),
        \qquad
        V = \pi\int_0^1 \left(x^2 - x^4\right)dx.
      </md>
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-washer-volume" label="check-washer-volume">
      <title>Try it</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate. Give a decimal to three places.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>V \approx</m> <fillin answer="0.419" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes" tolerance="0.002"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. <m>\pi\left(\frac13 - \frac15\right) = \frac{2\pi}{15} \approx 0.419</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-shifted-axis">
    <title>Distance to the axis matters</title>

    <p>
      Same region. Now spin it about the line <m>y = 2</m> instead. (The
      <q>Axis y = 2</q> example in the animation.)
    </p>
    <p>
      Which curve is farther from the axis now? The axis sits <em>above</em>
      the region. So the lower curve <m>y = x^2</m> is the farther one.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-shifted-radii" label="check-shifted-radii">
      <title>Find the radii</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the outer and inner radius as distances from <m>y = 2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          A radius is a distance, so it is (axis) minus (curve) here:
          <md>
            R(x) = 2 - x^2, \qquad r(x) = 2 - x.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <p>
      So
      <md>
        V = \pi\int_0^1 \left[(2 - x^2)^2 - (2 - x)^2\right]dx = \frac{8\pi}{15}.
      </md>
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-radius-is-distance">
      <title>Remember</title>
      <p>
        <q>Radius = the function</q> is a shortcut that only works when the axis
        is <m>y = 0</m>. The real rule: <alert>radius = distance from the axis of rotation.</alert>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-horizontal">
    <title>Horizontal slices: the same idea</title>

    <p>
      Nothing about <m>V = \int A</m> requires <m>dx</m>. If the axis is
      vertical, the slices are horizontal, and everything becomes a function of <m>y</m>:
      <md>
        V = \int_c^d A(y)\,dy.
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Take the region between <m>x = y^2</m> and <m>x = 4</m>,
      <m>-2 \le y \le 2</m>, and spin it about the <m>y</m>-axis. (The
      <q>About y-axis</q> example.) A horizontal slice runs from
      <m>x = y^2</m> out to <m>x = 4</m>, so
      <md>
        R(y) = 4, \qquad r(y) = y^2, \qquad
        V = \pi\int_{-2}^{2}\left(16 - y^4\right)dy.
      </md>
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-slice-direction">
      <title>Which way do I slice?</title>
      <p>
        For disks and washers, slices are perpendicular to the axis.
        Horizontal axis: vertical slices, <m>dx</m>.
        Vertical axis: horizontal slices, <m>dy</m>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-you-do">
    <title>You do: set up before you integrate</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-6-2-you-do-disk" label="ex-6-2-you-do-disk">
      <title>A disk</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rotate the region bounded by <m>y = \sqrt{x}</m>, <m>y = 0</m>, and
          <m>x = 4</m> about the <m>x</m>-axis. Find <m>r(x)</m>, <m>A(x)</m>,
          and the integral for <m>V</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>r(x) = \sqrt{x}</m>, so <m>A(x) = \pi(\sqrt{x})^2 = \pi x</m>, and
          <m>V = \pi\int_0^4 x\,dx = 8\pi</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-6-2-you-do-washer" label="ex-6-2-you-do-washer">
      <title>A washer</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rotate the region between <m>y = 4</m> and <m>y = x^2</m>,
          <m>-2 \le x \le 2</m>, about the <m>x</m>-axis. Find <m>R(x)</m>,
          <m>r(x)</m>, <m>A(x)</m>, and the integral for <m>V</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>R(x) = 4</m> and <m>r(x) = x^2</m>, so
          <m>A(x) = \pi(4)^2 - \pi(x^2)^2 = \pi(16 - x^4)</m> and
          <m>V = \pi\int_{-2}^{2}(16 - x^4)\,dx</m>. By symmetry,
          <m>2\pi\int_0^2 (16 - x^4)\,dx</m> is also correct.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-6-2-shifted" label="ex-6-2-shifted">
      <title>Less scaffolding</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rotate the region bounded by <m>y = x^2</m> and <m>y = 4</m> about the
          line <m>y = 5</m>. Set up the integral. Do not evaluate.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The curves meet at <m>x = \pm 2</m>. The axis is above the region, so
          the lower curve <m>y = x^2</m> is farther: <m>R(x) = 5 - x^2</m>. The
          line <m>y = 4</m> is closer: <m>r(x) = 5 - 4 = 1</m>.
          <md>
            V = \pi\int_{-2}^{2}\left[(5 - x^2)^2 - 1\right]dx.
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-mistake">
    <title>Find the mistake</title>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-2-error-analysis" label="check-6-2-error-analysis">
      <title>Error analysis</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Three students rotate the region between <m>y = x</m> and
          <m>y = x^2</m>, <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, about the <m>x</m>-axis.
          Which setup is correct?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p>Student A: <m>V = \pi\int_0^1 (x - x^2)^2\,dx</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Student A used <m>x - x^2</m> as a radius. That is the vertical
              thickness of the flat region, not a distance from the axis. The
              cross-section is a washer, not a disk.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Student B: <m>V = \pi\int_0^1 (x^2 - x^4)\,dx</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Yes. Outer disk minus inner disk: <m>\pi x^2 - \pi x^4</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement><p>Student C: <m>V = \int_0^1 \pi x^2 - \pi x^4\,dx</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The idea is right, but the notation is not. Without parentheses,
              the <m>dx</m> only belongs to the last term. Write
              <m>\int_0^1 \left(\pi x^2 - \pi x^4\right)dx</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-2-checklist">
    <title>A setup checklist</title>

    <p>
      Before you integrate, ask:
      <ol>
        <li><p>What is the axis of rotation?</p></li>
        <li><p>Should my slices be vertical or horizontal?</p></li>
        <li><p>What does one slice become when it spins?</p></li>
        <li><p>What is the outer radius? Is there an inner radius?</p></li>
        <li><p>Are my radii actual distances from the axis?</p></li>
        <li><p>What are my limits, and does my differential match the slices?</p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-6-2-main-idea">
      <title>Main idea</title>
      <p>
        Draw, slice, radius, area, integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        The main formula is not the disk formula or the washer formula. It is
        <md>
          V = \int_a^b A(x)\,dx.
        </md>
        Disks and washers are just two ways of finding <m>A(x)</m>.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise xml:id="check-6-2-exit" label="check-6-2-exit">
      <title>Exit ticket</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The axis of rotation is <m>y = 7</m> and a curve sits at <m>y = 2</m>.
          What radius does that curve give?
        </p>
        <p>
          radius <m>=</m> <fillin answer="5" mode="number"/>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <evaluation>
        <evaluate>
          <test correct="yes">
            <numcmp use-answer="yes"/>
            <feedback><p>Yes. Distance from the axis: <m>7 - 2 = 5</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
          <test>
            <numcmp value="2"/>
            <feedback><p>That is the height of the curve. The radius is its distance from the axis <m>y = 7</m>.</p></feedback>
          </test>
        </evaluate>
      </evaluation>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
